Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum dimension of the fixed-point set of $A - B$ for distinct proper rigid transformations $A, B \in \operatorname{SE}(n)$.

## Proof

**Setup.** Every element of $\operatorname{SE}(n)$ has the form $T(x) = Rx + t$ with $R \in \operatorname{SO}(n)$ and $t \in \mathbb{R}^n$. Write $A(x) = R_A x + t_A$, $B(x) = R_B x + t_B$. The equation $A(x) = B(x)$ becomes
$$(R_A - R_B)\,x = t_B - t_A. \tag{1}$$

**Case 1: $R_A = R_B$, $t_A \neq t_B$.** Then (1) becomes $0 = t_B - t_A \neq 0$, which has no solution. So $\operatorname{Eq}(A,B) = \emptyset$.

**Case 2: $R_A \neq R_B$.** Set $Q = R_B^{-1} R_A \in \operatorname{SO}(n)$, so $Q \neq I$ and $R_A - R_B = R_B(Q - I)$. Since $R_B$ is invertible, $\operatorname{rank}(R_A - R_B) = \operatorname{rank}(Q - I)$.

**Rank analysis of $Q - I$.** Since $Q \in \operatorname{SO}(n)$ is orthogonal, it is diagonalizable over $\mathbb{C}$. Its eigenvalues are $1$ with some multiplicity $k \geq 0$, together with conjugate pairs $e^{\pm i\theta_j}$ ($\theta_j \neq 0$), where $k + 2m = n$ and $m$ is the number of non-trivial rotation pairs. The eigenvalues of $Q - I$ are $0$ (multiplicity $k$) and $e^{\pm i\theta_j} - 1 \neq 0$ (each pair contributing rank 2). Since $Q \neq I$, at least one pair is non-trivial, so $m \geq 1$ and
$$\operatorname{rank}(Q - I) = 2m = n - k \geq 2.$$
The minimum rank $2$ is achieved when $k = n - 2$, i.e., $Q$ is a single non-trivial plane rotation fixing an $(n{-}2)$-dimensional subspace.

**Consistency.** The system (1) is consistent iff $t_B - t_A \in \operatorname{range}(R_A - R_B)$. When consistent, the solution set is an affine subspace of dimension
$$n - \operatorname{rank}(R_A - R_B) = n - 2m \leq n - 2.$$

**Achieving the bound $n - 2$.** Take $R_A = I$ and $R_B$ = rotation by angle $\theta \neq 0$ in the $(x_1, x_2)$-plane (fixing $x_3, \ldots, x_n$). Set $t_A = t_B = 0$. Then $A \neq B$ (since $R_A \neq R_B$), and
$$\operatorname{Eq}(A,B) = \ker(I - R_B) = \{x : x_1 = x_2 = 0\},$$
which is the $(n{-}2)$-dimensional coordinate subspace $\operatorname{span}(e_3, \ldots, e_n)$.

**Conclusion.** The maximum dimension of $\operatorname{Eq}(A, B)$ over all distinct $A, B \in \operatorname{SE}(n)$ is

$$\boxed{n - 2}.$$

